WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.300
Four small moves: how a strong relationship is built.

2
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:09.230
Salma is three months into her first job. At the next desk, Yudha puts down the phone and sighs.

3
00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:15.100
A sigh like that is a small request for attention. The psychologist John Gottman calls it a bid for connection.

4
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:23.340
She can turn toward it, miss it, or brush it off. Trust is built in these seconds, many times a day.

5
00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:32.090
She turns toward it. One conversation, four moves: attention, vulnerability, empathy and compassion.

6
00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:41.240
Phone face down, and she lets him finish. Then something true about herself: I froze on my first call too. Before any advice, what she heard: sounds like it hit harder than you expected. And she closes with an act: shall we rehearse the next one at four?

7
00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:47.840
Everyone agrees with the four words. Few practise them in an ordinary week. So pick one person and one move.

8
00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:56.380
A team learns fastest when people can speak up and admit mistakes. From the junior seat it starts with asking, owning a mistake early, and thanking the person who corrected you.

9
00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:06.255
On Tuesday her supervisor adds a second task to a full week. A yes she cannot keep will hurt the relationship more than an honest limit. So she states a boundary in three parts: a commitment, a limit and an option.

10
00:01:06.655 --> 00:01:13.655
Trust is built in seconds, with one person at a time. A boundary stated early and kindly protects the relationship.

11
00:01:14.055 --> 00:01:18.455
Now it is your turn. Name one person, and choose one of the four moves to practise with them this week.
